\strong{Models.} Two approaches are available, chosen with the option \code{model} of
\code{\link{ranktreecontrol}}.

With \code{model = "kemeny"} (default) the tree is the recursive partitioning method of D'Ambrosio and
Heiser (2016): the impurity of a node is based on Kemeny distances, and the class of a node is its median
ranking computed with \code{consrank}. With \code{impurity = "pairwise"} (default) the impurity is the sum
of the Kemeny distances between all the pairs of rankings within the node, divided by its size, as in
D'Ambrosio and Heiser (2016): the splits do not depend on the median rankings, so the tree is the same
with any \code{algorithm}. With \code{impurity = "median"} the impurity of a node is the sum of the Kemeny
distances of its rankings from its median ranking (\code{impur} is this sum divided by the size of the
node, i.e. the mean distance from the median, and \code{wimpur} is the sum divided by the sample size),
and the best split maximises the decrease of this sum, divided by the sample size. The median rankings
of the two children of every candidate split are computed from their combined input matrices (updated
by cumulative sums over the cutting points of ordered predictors), so the class of a node is exactly the
median ranking used for its impurity. With a heuristic \code{algorithm} the decrease of a split can be
slightly negative.

\strong{Objects not ranked.} With \code{model = "kemeny"} the objects not ranked by a judge are coded as
\code{NA}, and the option \code{unranked} of \code{\link{ranktreecontrol}} says how they are treated. With
\code{unranked = "last"} (default) they are tied in the position after the last object ranked by the judge, as
in D'Ambrosio and Heiser (2016), and the rankings stored in the tree (\code{Y}) are completed in this way. With
\code{unranked = "unknown"} (only with \code{impurity = "median"}) they come after the ranked objects, but their
order is unknown: a pair of two objects not ranked by a judge is not observed, and it counts neither as a
preference nor as a tie. The combined input matrix of a node then counts, for each pair, only the judges who rank
at least one of its objects, and the impurity is the sum of the Kemeny distances from the median ranking computed
on the observed pairs; \code{tau} and \code{error} of the nodes are the averaged \eqn{\tau_X}{tau_X} and the
normalized Kemeny distance on the observed pairs, and so are \code{prunecost = "error"} and the measures of
\code{validatetree}. For top-k lists ("choose and rank k objects out of n") this convention uses all the
information given by the judges, and nothing more: the combined input matrix is the average of the matrices
obtained by completing each top-k list in all the possible ways. The rankings stored in the tree keep the
\code{NA}.

The consensus of the nodes can be constrained with one of the options \code{nbuckets} (median bucket order with
a fixed number of buckets), \code{bucketsizes} (median bucket order with a pattern of bucket sizes) and \code{topk}
(median top-k list) of \code{\link{ranktreecontrol}}, computed by \code{\link[ConsRank]{mcbo}} and
\code{\link[ConsRank]{consranktopk}} from the combined input matrix of the node. The class of each node is then
the constrained consensus, and so are the measures based on it (\code{tau}, \code{error}, \code{validatetree},
\code{predict}). With \code{impurity = "pairwise"} the tree is the same as without the constraint and only the
classes change; with \code{impurity = "median"} the impurity is the sum of the distances from the constrained
consensus, so the constraint drives the choice of the splits. No probability
model is assumed, and the rankings can contain ties: the Kemeny distance is twice the Kendall distance for
complete rankings, and is defined also for rankings with ties.

With \code{model = "mallows"} the tree is the distance-based tree of Lee and Yu (2010): a Mallows model with
Kendall distance is fitted by maximum likelihood within each node. The class of the node is the modal
ranking of the model and the impurity is its deviance (\eqn{-2} times the log-likelihood) divided by the
size of the node. The best split maximises the decrease in deviance
\eqn{D_{father} - D_{left} - D_{right}}{D_father - D_left - D_right}, divided by the sample size (so
\code{decrmin} is on this scale), and splits leaving in a child node fewer than \code{minprop} times the
judges of the father node (default 10\%, as in Lee and Yu, 2010) are not considered. The cost-complexity
pruning uses the deviance of the leaves. The nodes contain also \code{lambda} (the precision parameter of
the Mallows model) and \code{deviance}; \code{goodness} contains the deviance, the log-likelihood, the
