\code{print}, \code{summary}, \code{plot} and \code{validatetree} report the measures of the Mallows models.

The \code{"mallows"} model handles complete rankings and top-k partial rankings ("pick k out of M", as
Inglehart's items in the \code{EVS} data). The unranked objects are coded as \code{NA}, or tied in the last
position, and are assumed to be in unknown order: the probability of a top-k ranking is the sum of the
probabilities of all its compatible complete rankings, as in Lee and Yu (2010). It is computed exactly in
closed form (Fligner and Verducci, 1986), so the number of unranked objects is not limited. Rankings with
ties among the ranked objects are not allowed (an error is returned): a tie expresses indifference, which a
model on permutations cannot represent. The rankings stored in the tree (\code{Y}) are in standard form:
ranked objects in positions 1, ..., k and unranked objects tied in position k + 1.

The modal ranking of a node is the Kemeny median ranking restricted to permutations of the pairs ordered
